  <item>
    <title>Atlantic Hurricane Season Remains Most Tranquil Since 1941</title>
    <link>https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2026/09/04/884092.htm</link>
    <description>September is usually the month when deadly Atlantic hurricanes menace coastal communities from Nicaragua to Newfoundland, but the world’s second-largest ocean hasn’t been this docile since 1941. That’s the lowest since 1941, which had just six storms, including more ferocious tempests than 2026 has generated so far, according to National Hurricane Center records. Dry air, a stable atmosphere and storm ripping wind shear are suppressing every system that emerges, fulfilling predictions that a strong El Niño will make it a quiet hurricane season.</description>
    <pubDate>Fri, 04 Sep 2026 15:33:27 +0000</pubDate>
    <guid>https://www.insurancejournal.com/news/national/2026/09/04/884092.htm</guid>
    <category>General</category>
